## Why This Is Asked

Assesses the ability to design intelligent, cost-aware routing systems for multi-module AI architectures in real-time environments, requiring consideration of performance trade-offs, safety constraints, and practical implementation details.

## Key Concepts

- Latency budgeting and real-time performance optimization
- Cost-aware routing and resource allocation
- Safety gating and data sensitivity handling
- Modular prompt orchestration and fallback mechanisms
- Policy-based decision making with precedence rules

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt, signals) {
  const { budget, latency, safety, data_sensitivity } = signals;

  // Safety-first routing
  if (data_sensitivity > 0.7 || safety > 0.5) {
    return {
      module: 'SafetyGuard',
      plan: ['analyze_content', 'apply_filters', 'return_sanitized'],
      payload: { prompt, safety_score: safety, sensitivity_level: data_sensitivity }
    };
  }

  // Cost-optimized routing
  if (budget < 0.3 || latency < 100) {
    return {
      module: 'LightSummarizer',
      plan: ['extract_key_points', 'generate_summary'],
      payload: { prompt, max_tokens: 150 }
    };
  }

  // Default to comprehensive processing
  return {
    module: 'CitationEmbedder',
    plan: ['analyze_content', 'extract_citations', 'generate_embeddings'],
    payload: { prompt, include_sources: true }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a real-time, multi-tenant prompt routing system with integrated safety guards and dynamic policy management.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic policy store with hot-reload capabilities without redeployment
- PII/secrets detection and safe fallback mechanisms
- Latency budgeting and strategic caching
- Edge-case handling: conflicting signals, missing authentication

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal router prototype
const policy = { maxLatencyMs: 120, allowedTopics: ['query','analysis'] };
function route(prompt, userAuth) {
  const t0 = performance.now();

  // PII detection
  if (containsPII(prompt)) return { module: 'SafetyGuard', reason: 'PII detected' };

  // Policy validation
  if (!validatePolicy(prompt, policy)) return { module: 'ActionOrchestrator', reason: 'Policy constraint' };

  // Auth check
  if (!userAuth) return { module: 'SafetyGuard', reason: 'Missing authentication' };

  // Default routing
  return { module: 'PromptParser', reason: 'Standard query' };
}
```

## Implementation Considerations

- Redis for policy hot-reloads without service restart
- In-memory caching for frequently accessed policy rules
- Circuit breakers for policy store failures
- Metrics tracking for latency compliance

## Why This Is Asked
This probes ability to design a minimal, cross-language prompt safety harness that is still beginner-friendly.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual prompt handling
- Lightweight dictionary-based translation
- Safety checks for prompts and patterns
- Per-language reporting and remediation
- JSON payload design and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function testPrompt(enPrompt) {
  const dict = { es: { export: "exportar", emails: "correo" }, fr: { export: "exporter", emails: "courriel" }, zh: { export: "导出", emails: "电子邮件" } };
  const langs = ["es","fr","zh"];
  const unsafe = ["system prompt","secret","privileged"];
  const translate = (p, lang) => {
    let t = p;
    Object.entries(dict[lang] || {}).forEach(([enWord, trans]) => {
      t = t.replace(new RegExp("\\b" + enWord + "\\b","gi"), trans);
    });
    return t;
  };
  const results = langs.map(l => {
    const t = translate(enPrompt, l);
    const issues = unsafe.filter(s => t.toLowerCase().includes(s));
    return { lang: l, prompt: t, ok: issues.length === 0, issues };
  });
  return { original: enPrompt, results };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale to more languages and handle ambiguous terms?
- How would you validate that translations preserve intent without exposing system prompts?

## Why This Is Asked

Interacting across tenants requires real-time policy-compliant routing. The candidate must design a routing/policy layer that respects ABAC, handles sensitive content, and balances safety with latency, all while exposing auditable decisions.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt intent extraction and sensitive-term detection
- ABAC policy evaluation against user attributes
- Model variant selection under policy constraints
- Auditing: fingerprinting, latency, policy_score, route trace
- Lightweight prototype with mock models for realism

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt, tenant) {
  const intents = /summarize|export|analyze|decrypt/.test(prompt) ? 'specialized' : 'general';
  const sensitive = /PII|password|secret/.test(prompt);
  const policy = (tenant.policies || []).find(p => p.action === 'route');
  const model = policy && policy.allows && !sensitive ? 'fast-safe' : 'strict';
  const fingerprint = hash(prompt + tenant.id);
  return { finalPrompt: prompt, model, fingerprint };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy updates and cache invalidation?
- What metrics would you collect to detect model drift after routing decisions?
